22
00:01:12.539 --> 00:01:16.810
The definition is through DSPy
signature. In short language,

23
00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:20.380
Signature defines the input-output fields

24
00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:24.184
along with types
and annotations of the LM interaction.

25
00:01:24.284 --> 00:01:27.887
There are two ways of defining a DSPy
signature,

26
00:01:28.288 --> 00:01:31.424
class-based signature and stream-based
signature.

27
00:01:31.791 --> 00:01:34.794
The first way is class-based signature.

28
00:01:34.894 --> 00:01:38.264
All you need to do
is subclassing from DSPy dot signature.

29
00:01:38.765 --> 00:01:40.266
And the mark input fields,

30
00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:43.336
by DSPy dot input field.
And output fields

31
00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:48.336
by the output field. And optionally, you
can provide type and annotation to each field.

32
00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:52.178
There are five important parts
of a class-based

33
00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:52.846
signature.

34
00:01:54.514 --> 00:01:55.515
The red box,

35
00:01:55.515 --> 00:01:58.384
which is the doctrine of the signature
class,

36
00:01:58.384 --> 00:02:02.555
is a signature instruction which defines
the purpose of the LM call.

37
00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:07.660
Like a brief overview of your task, this
usually only requires a few sentences,

38
00:02:07.994 --> 00:02:11.798
but if you already have an existing prompt
and don't want to simplify,

39
00:02:12.098 --> 00:02:15.101
you can paste the whole prompt
into a docstring.

40
00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:18.138
The orange box is the field name.

41
00:02:18.138 --> 00:02:22.242
You will use this name to carry
input data and access the output data.

42
00:02:22.609 --> 00:02:27.080
The blue box simply tells the program
if there's an input field or output field.

43
00:02:28.014 --> 00:02:31.117
And the purple box carries
actual information about the field

44
00:02:31.584 --> 00:02:35.121
and it's useful when the field name
is not self-explanatory.

45
00:02:35.522 --> 00:02:38.892
Lastly, the green box carries
the type information,

46
00:02:39.225 --> 00:02:40.693
which could be unimplemented

47
00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:44.230
Python types who are behind in costume
class or pydantic models.

48
00:02:44.697 --> 00:02:48.535
This is mostly useful for output fields.
If you specify the type

49
00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:52.338
when you access the output fields,
the value will automatically be

50
00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:53.773
